// Website-owned directory of the public Knowledge Islands ecosystem.
//
// This file answers one question: what are the projects, what is each one for,
// and who owns it. It is the single registry behind `/projects/`, including the
// released command-line tools, which are projects that happen to ship a binary
// and therefore carry release and install fields the others leave out. Keeping
// them in a second file preserved a split in the data that the reader could see
// as two indistinguishable sections (KI-WEBSITE-SITE-020).
//
// It is still a directory, not an artefact host. Each tool repository remains
// the authority for its executable behaviour, release artefacts, checksums and
// installer script; the Homebrew tap remains the authority for its formula. The
// site promises only that `/projects/<slug>/` describes the tool and that
// `/install/<slug>` redirects to the installer published at the declared
// immutable release tag.
//
// Only public repositories appear. A private or internally operated repository
// is deliberately absent; the directory is a public surface and must not leak
// the existence of work that is not itself public.
//
// Fields
//   slug         route segment for `/projects/<slug>/`, and the card anchor
//   name         the repository as its owners name it
//   kind         principal | capability | standard | tool | mcp | platform
//   tagline      one short sentence for cards and page headers
//   description  a fuller paragraph for the project page
//   role         the authority this project holds in the ecosystem
//   usage        how a reader actually consumes it today, stated honestly
//   repository   canonical source repository
//   availability published | source (see docs/guides/developer/projects-directory.md)
//   route        an existing site page that is already this project's home;
//                omit it and `/projects/<slug>/` is generated instead
//   icon         KI symbol name from src/_includes/macros/icons.njk
//   accent       symbol-circle accent: gold | teal | forest
//
// Every entry without a `route` gets a generated page, so it must also carry
// the five reader-facing fields that page is built from (KI-WEBSITE-SITE-023). They
// answer, in order, the questions a reader deciding whether to adopt something
// actually asks:
//   problem      what problem this solves — the reason it exists at all
//   audience     who has that problem, stated so a reader can rule themselves out
//   capabilities 3 or more concrete things it does, as plain sentences
//   state        what state it is in, honestly, including what is not done
//   limits       what it deliberately does not do, and what stays elsewhere
//
// `usage` answers the fifth question — how to start — and predates them.
// Plain prose only: these render as text, so Markdown syntax would show as
// literal characters. `verify-projects.ts` fails a generated entry missing any
// of them, so an unfilled page cannot reach `dist/`.
//
// Released tools (`kind: 'tool'`) carry six more, and every other kind must
// carry none of them:
//   version      published release this registry currently advertises
//   maturity     stable | preview | experimental
//   formula      Homebrew formula in knowledgeislands/tap, or null when untapped
//   installer    immutable installer target the machine route redirects to
//   manual       human manual at the declared version
//   changelog    release history at the declared version
//
// Advancing a tool entry is a release follow-up performed by the tool's own
// repository through `scripts/sync-tool-release.ts`; `bun run verify:routes`
// and `bun run verify:projects` gate the declaration before deployment.
[
  // ── Principal knowledge bases ───────────────────────────────────────────
  {
    slug: 'ki-principal',
    name: 'ki-principal',
    kind: 'principal',
    tagline: 'The canonical record of the Knowledge Islands philosophy and conceptual model.',
    description:
      'Knowledge Islands Principal is where the approach is developed and proved before anything is built on it. It holds the philosophy, the conceptual model, and the worked record that shows the model surviving contact with real knowledge work — and it does so without mandating any particular tooling implementation.',
    problem:
      'An approach to knowledge work that lives in slide decks and habits cannot be argued with, and cannot be inherited. Knowledge Islands Principal is the opposite arrangement: one public knowledge base where the philosophy and the conceptual model are developed in the open and kept as a living record, so anything built downstream can be traced back to a stated idea rather than to an assumption nobody wrote down.',
    audience:
      'Read it if you want to understand why Knowledge Islands is shaped the way it is before deciding whether any of the tooling is worth adopting, or if you are working out how to hold your own approach explicitly. It is not a manual, and it will not tell you which command to run.',
    capabilities: [
      'A Philosophy pillar: the island metaphor, the conceptual model, and the realisation material that connects the two.',
      'A Technē pillar carrying the conceptual tool-ecosystem map the implementation repositories are arranged by.',
      'An Engineering Practice pillar: the canonical engineering discipline — architecture, operating models and technology posture — adopted from the retired Techne Principal.',
      'An Aesthetics pillar holding the symbol library, logo, diagrams and visual style this website is built from.',
      'The ecosystem decision GDR-KI-PRINCIPAL-006, cited across the estate, which fixes who owns what.',
      'A Streams area carrying the live working record, so the reasoning is visible rather than only its conclusions.'
    ],
    state:
      'Public and actively developed, in continuous revision rather than versioned releases. The distilled account of the philosophy and the model is published on this site; the repository holds the full working record behind it.',
    limits:
      'It mandates no implementation. Nothing here constrains any repository\'s code, and a change made in Arcadia obliges no tool to follow — KI Specifications exists for the cases where obligation is actually wanted. It is also not a tutorial: there is no getting-started path, because there is nothing here to install.',
    role: 'Canonical source of the philosophy and conceptual model. It informs every other repository and mandates none of their implementations.',
    usage: 'A public Markdown knowledge base. Read it in the repository, or read the distilled account under Philosophy and Model on this site.',
    repository: 'https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-principal',
    availability: 'source',
    icon: 'island',
    accent: 'gold'
  },

  // ── Agentic capabilities ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  {
    slug: 'ki-agentic-harness',
    name: 'ki-agentic-harness',
    kind: 'capability',
    tagline: 'The canonical source of reusable agentic capabilities and compatible harnesses.',
    description:
      'A capability here is a typed published member of a compatible harness: a skill, an agent, an MCP server, a hook, an eval, or a future registered kind. The harness defines what each capability contains and what it means. It does not implement the ki executable, and it does not originate portable normative contracts.',
    problem:
      'Working conventions that live only in prose erode. An agent applies them if it happens to be told to, and nobody finds out when it was not; a reviewer catches what they have time to read. Holding each standard as a skill that registers its own mechanical checks makes the clear-cut cases deterministic, so attention goes to the parts that genuinely need judgement.',
    audience:
      'Install it if you want the Knowledge Islands standards applied and checked in your own repositories. It is what ki bootstrap installs, and what ki repo audit resolves its checks from — you do not read this repository to use it. Outside a Knowledge Islands setup, npx skills add knowledgeislands/ki-agentic-harness installs individual skills directly.',
    capabilities: [
      'Skills — reusable standards, each registering the operations that audit and conform a target against it.',
      'Subagents — focused specialist roles an agent can delegate a piece of work to.',
      'Hooks — optional automation around a coding session, such as plan-mode lifecycle and stale Git-lock recovery.',
      'Evals — scenarios that check a skill changes behaviour, rather than only that it installed.',
      'A declared capability-prefix namespace, which is how two installed harnesses are stopped from both claiming to supply the same skill.'
    ],
    state:
      'The canonical harness: it is what ki bootstrap installs, and it supplies the core user skills an installation needs to describe itself. It cannot be uninstalled, because an installation without it could not report what it has. The skills are the most built-out part; the MCP shelf is scaffolded, and the servers it will hold currently live in their own repositories.',
    limits:
      'It publishes capability content and installs nothing. Acquisition, verification and activation belong to the ki CLI, and which capabilities govern a repository belongs to that repository\'s own declaration — installing a harness activates nothing. Public visibility does not grant a licence to use, copy or redistribute.',
    role: 'Canonical source of capability content and semantics, and of the compatible-harness contract that ki installs against.',
    usage: 'Install a compatible harness with the ki CLI, then activate only the capabilities a piece of work needs.',
    repository: 'https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-agentic-harness',
    availability: 'source',
    icon: 'archipelago',
    accent: 'teal'
  },

  // ── Portable standards ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  {
    slug: 'ki-specifications',
    name: 'ki-specifications',
    kind: 'standard',
    tagline: 'The canonical source of normative, portable Knowledge Islands contracts.',
    description:
      'KIPs, KIS documents, schemas, templates, conformance rules, reference examples, and the portable capability identity and inventory contracts. Specifications formalise concepts once they are proven and once implementations have supplied evidence; an Active KIS then governs every implementation claiming conformance within its scope.',
    problem:
      'A convention that exists only inside an implementation cannot be conformed to, argued with, or versioned — the code is simultaneously the rule and the only evidence of it. This repository is where a proven concept becomes a written contract that something else can claim conformance with, and be measured against.',
    audience:
      'Reach for it if you are implementing something that has to interoperate with Knowledge Islands, or proposing a change to a foundational concept rather than to a single tool. If you want to know how the tooling behaves today, the tool repositories and this site\'s guidance answer that faster.',
    capabilities: [
      'The KIP series: numbered proposals moving Draft to Review to Accepted, then Implemented once a specification exists.',
      'The KIS series: the normative specification an accepted proposal produces, moving Draft to Active once there is real implementation experience behind it.',
      'Schemas, templates, conformance rules and reference examples published alongside a specification.',
      'A documented process for raising a proposal and stewarding it through review, and registries that track both series.'
    ],
    state:
      'The process, the registries and the governance are in place; no KIP or KIS document is registered yet. The series start afresh at KIP-000001 and KIS-0001, so today this repository is the mechanism rather than the corpus. It is MIT licensed, text and schemas alike.',
    limits:
      'It formalises what implementations have already proved rather than designing ahead of them, which is why it is deliberately empty rather than speculatively full. The corpus is not published on this site: with nothing Active, there would be nothing to read.',
    role: 'Canonical source of portable normative contracts. Active specifications constrain the harness and the CLI; neither of them constrains a specification.',
    usage: 'Read the canonical documents and schemas in the repository. The corpus is not yet published on this site.',
    repository: 'https://github.com/knowledgeislands/ki-specifications',
    availability: 'source',
    icon: 'lighthouse',
    accent: 'gold'
  },

  // ── Command-line tools ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  {
    slug: 'ki',
    name: 'ki',
    kind: 'tool',
    tagline: 'The Knowledge Islands command-line interface.',
    description:
      'Installs verified compatible harnesses, activates skills in explicit scopes, and hosts the native repository operations that govern a Knowledge Islands checkout.',
    problem:
      'Agent capabilities usually arrive as files copied into place, after which nobody can say which version is where or which rules a given repository is actually subject to. ki replaces copying with declaration: it installs verified harnesses, activates capabilities in explicit scopes, and runs exactly the checks a repository declares — so the governance travels with the code rather than with your machine.',
    audience:
      'You want this if you work across more than one repository with an agent, or with anyone else, and the answer to "what governs this code" needs to be the same on every clone. For a single personal project with no rules to share, it is more machinery than the problem needs.',
    capabilities: [
      'bootstrap: one command that detects your installed agent runtimes, installs the verified canonical harness, and links the core user skills.',
      'skill add and repo skill add: activate a capability for you, or for a repository, in an explicit scope that is never implied.',
      'repo educate, audit and conform: explain what the declared skills expect, check it read-only, then apply the repairs.',
      'manage doctor, diag, outdated and repair: what is installed, what is behind, what is broken, and what to do about it.',
      'A hundred and nineteen commands across eighteen groups in total, listed in full on this site rather than left to the manual.'
    ],
    state:
      'Published and labelled preview. The command surface is settled enough to build habits on — bootstrap, skill activation and the audit loop have been stable for several releases — and the version number is the honest signal that it is still moving.',
    limits:
      'It runs only the operations a repository\'s declared skills register, from harnesses whose payloads verified. It will not run a repository-local wrapper script, a copied rubric runner, a package alias, or a checkout that happens to be nearby. That is what makes "the audit passed" a precise claim and a narrow one: it says nothing about rules nobody declared. It originates no capability content and no standard.',
    role: 'Canonical implementation of the ki command surface. It installs and governs; it originates neither capability content nor portable standards.',
    usage: 'Install it from the Homebrew tap or the pinned installer below, then run ki bootstrap to install a verified harness and activate the core user skills.',
    repository: 'https://github.com/knowledgeislands/tools-ki',
    availability: 'published',
