  // ── MCP servers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  {
    slug: 'mcp-ki-kb-fs',
    name: 'mcp-ki-kb-fs',
    kind: 'mcp',
    tagline: 'Give an agent scoped read and write access to local knowledge-base directories.',
    description:
      'One registration serves many knowledge bases: the environment declares alias-to-path pairs and every tool call names the base it acts in. Each path is validated twice — lexically, then through realpath — so neither a traversal nor a symlink can reach outside the declared root, and no call under one alias can wander into a sibling base. Dotfiles and repository metadata stay hidden from ordinary tools. No network, no authentication.',
    problem:
      'An agent that can write notes needs somewhere bounded to write them. A general filesystem server gives it your whole disk; this one gives it exactly the knowledge-base directories you declared, with the boundary enforced on every call rather than trusted to a prompt.',
    audience:
      'You want this if you keep knowledge bases as Markdown on disk and want an agent reading and writing in them directly — particularly if there are several, since one registration serves them all.',
    capabilities: [
      'Read, write, list and delete notes across every declared base.',
      'Create folders and rename entries, so the agent can restructure rather than only edit.',
      'Report which aliases are declared and what each one resolves to, so the agent can discover its own boundaries.',
      'Name the base on every call, which is what lets one registration serve many knowledge bases at once.'
    ],
    state:
      'Public source, not published to any package registry. Clone it, build it, and register the built server with your MCP client. The path validation and the alias model are the settled parts; the tool surface is deliberately small.',
    limits:
      'Every path is validated twice, lexically and then through realpath, so neither a traversal nor a symlink reaches outside the declared root and no call under one alias wanders into a sibling base. Dotfiles and repository metadata stay hidden from ordinary tools. It makes no network request and performs no authentication: it reads and writes notes, and holds no knowledge of its own.',
    role: 'The knowledge-base filesystem adapter. It reads and writes notes; it holds no knowledge of its own.',
    usage: 'Not yet published to a registry. Clone the repository, build it, and register the built server with your MCP client.',
    repository: 'https://github.com/knowledgeislands/mcp-ki-kb-fs',
    availability: 'source',
    icon: 'book',
    accent: 'teal'
  },
  {
    slug: 'mcp-git-audit',
    name: 'mcp-git-audit',
    kind: 'mcp',
    tagline: 'Audit a whole tree of Git repositories, read-only until an operator says otherwise.',
    description:
      'Walks a tree of checkouts and reports branch, working-tree status, ahead and behind counts, and last-commit metadata for each. The walk and the per-repository git calls are split into separate tools, so one cheap filesystem scan can be cached and re-audited many times. Remote and branch mutations exist but stay hidden until an operator raises the access level, and every walked path is validated against a configured allow-list of safe roots.',
    problem:
      'Asking an agent about thirty checkouts means thirty status calls and no overview, and giving it a shell to do that with means giving it the ability to change them. This server reports the state of a whole tree — branch, working-tree status, ahead and behind counts, last commit — and is read-only until someone deliberately says otherwise.',
    audience:
      'You want this if you keep a wide working set and want an agent able to tell you what is dirty, what is unpushed and what has been abandoned, without handing it the ability to act on any of it.',
    capabilities: [
      'A cheap filesystem scan that finds every repository under the allowed roots, separated from the git calls so it can be cached and re-audited many times.',
      'A per-repository audit: branch, working-tree status, ahead and behind counts, and last-commit metadata.',
      'A closer look at one repository, including its diff, when the summary raises a question.',
      'Fetch, pull, push, commit and remote management, present but hidden until an operator raises the access level.'
    ],
    state:
      'Public source, not published to any package registry. Clone it, build it, and register the built server with your MCP client. The scan-and-audit split and the access model are the settled parts.',
    limits:
      'Read-only unless an operator says otherwise: the access level defaults to read, and the mutating tools are not registered at all until it is raised. Every walked path is validated against a configured allow-list of safe roots, so it cannot wander. It observes repositories; it governs none of them and knows nothing about the forge they came from.',
    role: 'A read-only-by-default Git observation adapter for wide working sets.',
