  {
    slug: 'mcp-housekeeping-claude',
    name: 'mcp-housekeeping-claude',
    kind: 'mcp',
    tagline: 'Audit and safely clean the filesystem state Claude applications accumulate.',
    description:
      'Codified audits across the three storage areas Claude apps leave on macOS: Claude Desktop and Cowork sessions, Claude Code state under the user\'s home, and VSCode chat sessions. Each audit step is its own tool, so an agent orchestrates the checks and writes the report. Every tool maps to an access level derived from its MCP annotations, and the default exposes read-only tools only.',
    problem:
      'Claude applications accumulate sessions, memory files, caches, artifacts and orphaned project state across three separate storage areas, and none of it is safe to delete blind — some of it is the only copy of a conversation you may still want. This server codifies the audits, so an agent can report what is actually there and prune only what is demonstrably obsolete.',
    audience:
      'You want this if you use Claude Desktop, Cowork, Claude Code or the VSCode extension heavily enough that disk usage, session sprawl or stale memory files have become a real cost.',
    capabilities: [
      'Claude Code: projects, sessions, memory files, storage summaries, obsolete-session detection, orphaned-project pruning and project relocation.',
      'Claude Desktop and Cowork: workspaces, sessions, artifacts health, plugin inventory, backups, reports and project cache status.',
      'VSCode chat sessions: workspaces, storage summary and obsolete-session detection.',
      'Session reading and checkpointing, so a conversation can be acquired before anything is pruned.',
      'Pruning tools for each area, separated from the audits that identify what is eligible.'
    ],
    state:
      'Public source, not published to any package registry, and macOS only — it addresses the paths those applications use there. Clone it, build it, and register the built server with your MCP client.',
    limits:
      'Each audit step is its own tool, so the agent orchestrates the checks and writes the report rather than the server deciding what matters. Every tool maps to an access level derived from its MCP annotations, and the default exposes read-only tools only, so pruning requires a deliberate change. It observes and prunes runtime state; it decides nothing about knowledge.',
    role: 'A local hygiene adapter for agent-runtime state. It observes and prunes; it decides nothing about knowledge.',
    usage: 'Not yet published to a registry. Clone the repository, build it, and register the built server with your MCP client.',
    repository: 'https://github.com/knowledgeislands/mcp-housekeeping-claude',
    availability: 'source',
    icon: 'current',
    accent: 'teal'
  },
